  "result": "All three tests pass with `python -m pytest -q`.\n\nI implemented `netgraph/graph.py` using **Approach A, adjacency sets**. Each node holds a `set` of neighbours, which gives O(1) duplicate detection and edge lookup, sorted output for `neighbors()` on demand, and memory proportional to nodes plus edges. I chose it because the spec's `neighbors()` and BFS `shortest_path()` are both degree-driven operations, and a matrix would spend n\u00b2 memory without making either one faster.\n\nOther decisions worth knowing:\n- Self-loops are stored once, so `edge_count()` counts them once and `neighbors(3)` returns `[3]`.\n- Node ids are validated on every call that takes one, raising `IndexError` for negatives, out-of-range values, and non-int ids. `Graph(n)` rejects a negative size with `ValueError`.\n- `shortest_path` runs BFS with a parent map and stops as soon as the target is discovered, returning `[u]` when the endpoints match and `None` when unreachable.\n\nNothing in `tests/` was modified.",
